1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,000
Hi, I'm Edwin, student number 25092450.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,900
The Atlantic widens by about 2–3 cm a year — about as fast as your fingernails grow.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:12,000
That's because the plates on either side of the mid-ocean ridge are moving apart.

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:17,000
One side's speed is the half-spreading rate; both sides together give the full-spreading rate.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:22,500
But no one can watch a plate for millions of years — so how do we measure its speed?

6
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:23,950
This is the mid-ocean ridge.

7
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:26,150
The two paper strips are the seafloor on either side of it.

8
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:29,350
When I pull the strips outward, new paper comes out from here,

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:32,050
just like new oceanic crust forming at the ridge.

10
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:36,350
As the rock cools, it records the direction of Earth's magnetic field at that time.

11
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:40,500
The magnetic patterns on the two strips are perfectly symmetric.

12
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:45,550
Now suppose this stripe is 100 km from the ridge and 5 million years old.

13
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:48,350
Then the half-spreading rate is 100 km ÷ 5 million years,

14
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:49,950
which is 2 cm per year.

15
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:55,250
So the seafloor works like a tape recorder, logging every flip of Earth's magnetic field.

16
00:00:55,650 --> 00:01:00,250
Read the stripes, and we can measure motion slower than your fingernails grow.
